WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.896
The final paper... not the final,

00:00:05.076 --> 00:00:08.216
but the last one I got interested in

00:00:08.416 --> 00:00:10.796
was written in the late forties

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:12.236
by a couple of German

00:00:12.236 --> 00:00:17.336
physicists at Göttingen and...

00:00:17.696 --> 00:00:22.136
they had been contacted by an eye doctor at the

00:00:22.136 --> 00:00:24.256
clinic and of course the way

00:00:24.376 --> 00:00:27.956
medicine works is that, you know, the local doctors

00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:30.596
will solve all the cases they know

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:33.236
how to solve and if they find something they

00:00:33.256 --> 00:00:35.756
can't help they refer it up the food chain

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:37.876
to the next doctor

00:00:37.876 --> 00:00:39.776
and so things keep moving up and so if you're

00:00:39.776 --> 00:00:42.104
the head of the eye clinic at Göttingen

00:00:42.104 --> 00:00:43.536
it means you're the most

00:00:43.556 --> 00:00:46.706
eminent eye doctor in the German-speaking world

00:00:46.706 --> 00:00:50.006
ok? So one day that this guy comes to

00:00:50.006 --> 00:00:52.616
work and in his waiting room is a man

00:00:52.616 --> 00:00:55.346
who's got "floaters." He can see

00:00:55.346 --> 00:00:57.716
little dark specks floating around in his

00:00:57.716 --> 00:00:59.786
vision which is a very common thing.

00:00:59.786 --> 00:01:01.915
So this is like if you're the most

00:01:01.915 --> 00:01:04.406
important dermatologist in the world and

00:01:04.406 --> 00:01:05.875
somebody comes to see you about a

00:01:05.875 --> 00:01:09.386
freckle. He looks into the

00:01:09.386 --> 00:01:12.086
guys eye and he sees tumbling around in

00:01:12.086 --> 00:01:14.906
the guy's vitreous humor he sees little tiny

00:01:14.906 --> 00:01:18.656
metal rods. They're obviously metal.

00:01:18.656 --> 00:01:22.526
They're obviously man-made and they're

00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:24.386
just floating around loose inside of his

00:01:24.386 --> 00:01:26.485
eyeball which is perfectly undamaged.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:30.086
There's no no entry wound nothing like that.

00:01:30.086 --> 00:01:34.316
So he's completely mystified.

00:01:34.316 --> 00:01:36.476
He asks the guy, "What did you do during the

00:01:36.476 --> 00:01:39.506
war were you in an explosion?

00:01:39.506 --> 00:01:41.366
Anything like that?"

00:01:41.366 --> 00:01:43.436
The guy goes, no, I didn't fight...

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.076
I was a civilian.

00:01:45.076 --> 00:01:47.836
Well, what do you do for a living?

00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:49.156
I drive a truck.

00:01:49.176 --> 00:01:52.316
Well, do you ever like maybe stick

00:01:52.316 --> 00:01:54.026
your head out the window or were you

00:01:54.026 --> 00:01:55.956
ever in a crash you know or anything like that?

00:01:55.956 --> 00:01:58.556
No, nothing like that, doctor.

00:01:58.756 --> 00:02:01.896
So eventually he had to give up

00:02:01.896 --> 00:02:05.096
and write it off as a mystery of science.

00:02:05.096 --> 00:02:09.036
But then a few months later,

00:02:09.036 --> 00:02:12.456
another guy shows up.

00:02:12.456 --> 00:02:15.786
Same symptoms exactly the same thing.

00:02:15.786 --> 00:02:17.856
No explanation for it. And then another

00:02:17.856 --> 00:02:18.606
and another.

00:02:18.606 --> 00:02:21.786
So he starts trying to put the pieces

00:02:21.786 --> 00:02:23.826
together and figure out what is the

00:02:23.826 --> 00:02:25.626
common thread among all of these people

00:02:25.626 --> 00:02:26.916
and turns out the one thing they all

00:02:26.916 --> 00:02:28.686
have in common is that they're drivers.

00:02:28.686 --> 00:02:34.236
Which by then met driving trucks, but

00:02:34.236 --> 00:02:37.656
during the war because we had targeted

00:02:37.656 --> 00:02:42.546
their... the Germans... fuel production

00:02:42.546 --> 00:02:45.096
there have been a lot of trucks lying

00:02:45.096 --> 00:02:48.006
around that couldn't be operated

00:02:48.006 --> 00:02:51.756
because there's no fuel. So they had gone back

00:02:51.756 --> 00:02:54.096
to hauling them behind animals and they had

00:02:54.096 --> 00:02:56.856
had to improvise harnesses and yokes and

00:02:56.856 --> 00:02:58.086
all that stuff because it was all gone

00:02:58.086 --> 00:03:01.536
nobody had used it for decades. And

00:03:01.536 --> 00:03:03.726
that was all easily invented from ropes and

00:03:03.726 --> 00:03:05.136
straps and whatever they had lying

00:03:05.136 --> 00:03:06.846
around. But the hard thing was

00:03:06.846 --> 00:03:09.876
getting whips. Because whips have some

00:03:09.876 --> 00:03:12.006
pretty specific properties that they have to

00:03:12.006 --> 00:03:13.955
have if they're going to crack correctly.

00:03:13.955 --> 00:03:17.556
But some genius figured out that

00:03:17.556 --> 00:03:19.536
one thing they had all over Germany by

00:03:19.536 --> 00:03:22.205
that point was collapsed buildings and

00:03:22.205 --> 00:03:23.886
a lot of those buildings had running down

00:03:23.886 --> 00:03:25.756
the outside a braided copper

00:03:25.756 --> 00:03:29.616
grounding cable and if you just went up

00:03:29.616 --> 00:03:30.876
to one of them and cut off the length

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:33.276
of that stuff and you know did the right

00:03:33.276 --> 00:03:35.406
motion you could get that thing to crack

00:03:35.406 --> 00:03:38.236
like a whip. A thing that a lot of

00:03:38.236 --> 00:03:41.336
novice whip crackers do is that they jerk

00:03:41.346 --> 00:03:43.866
the whip toward them to make it snap

00:03:43.866 --> 00:03:45.336
which is really dangerous because it

00:03:45.336 --> 00:03:47.735
comes back at you but it's a really easy

00:03:47.735 --> 00:03:51.545
way to get it to crack every time.

00:03:51.545 --> 00:03:54.366
So the doctor said...

00:03:54.366 --> 00:03:56.256
"Did it ever happen when you were doing that

00:03:56.256 --> 00:03:58.116
you like would feel you know

00:03:58.116 --> 00:04:00.726
one of these sharp pains in your eye?"

00:04:00.726 --> 00:04:02.645
And they said, yeah, that happens sometimes, but

00:04:02.645 --> 00:04:03.816
you know you just kind of blink it

00:04:03.816 --> 00:04:06.156
away. And few minutes later, you know,

00:04:06.156 --> 00:04:08.505
it's like nothing ever happened.

00:04:08.505 --> 00:04:11.295
So he goes running across the campus

00:04:11.295 --> 00:04:13.036
to the Physics department and he

00:04:13.036 --> 00:04:15.825
buttonholes a couple of physicists and he says, "Is

00:04:15.825 --> 00:04:17.806
it physically plausible that if you cracked

00:04:17.806 --> 00:04:20.325
a copper grounding cable like a whip

00:04:20.325 --> 00:04:22.426
that the tensile strength of the copper

00:04:22.426 --> 00:04:24.945
out at the end would get exceeded

00:04:24.945 --> 00:04:28.006
to the point where it would snap. And if so

00:04:28.006 --> 00:04:30.285
would the pieces be going fast

00:04:30.285 --> 00:04:32.445
enough that they could fly through the air

00:04:32.445 --> 00:04:33.856
for a few feet and penetrate

00:04:33.856 --> 00:04:36.696
somebody's eyeball and come to a stop

00:04:36.736 --> 00:04:38.476
inside the vitreous humor?"

00:04:38.476 --> 00:04:39.916
So the physicist, you know,

00:04:39.916 --> 00:04:42.836
I mean this is all that whole story is in a footnote!

00:04:42.836 --> 00:04:45.796
It's compressed into a footnote on

00:04:45.796 --> 00:04:48.466
page 1 of this paper. And then

00:04:48.466 --> 00:04:51.316
the rest of the paper is them working

00:04:51.316 --> 00:04:53.266
out all the physics of whip cracking and

00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:55.126
proving that you know all of those

00:04:55.126 --> 00:04:59.266
things could could happen. So as you can

00:04:59.266 --> 00:05:01.156
tell that's been a kind of a personal

00:05:01.156 --> 00:05:05.716
obsession of mine for...

00:05:05.716 --> 00:05:09.246
I'd say that story qualifies as stranger than fiction.

